axing a fan-favorite character is a  great way for writers to raise dramatic  stakes and audiences will generally let  them get away with it but sometimes a  character will be headed for the  chopping block only to get a last-minute  reprieve these are the times when  writers almost broke our hearts but then  chose not to and fan favorites who are  originally supposed to die got to live  on Daryl Dixon Walking Dead creator  Frank Darabont wrote the part of Daryl  Dixon especially for Norman Reedus the  character never appears in the comics  the show is based on Daryl was  originally supposed to do a few  appearances and then exit the way most  Walking Dead guests do as zombie food  but a funny thing happened fans loved  the character even though he was  initially totally unlikable  Darabont gave read us a chance to  develop the roll into something special  and over many seasons the character has  become so popular that Robert Kirkman  creator of the still ongoing comic  series has implied he'll eventually  write Darrell into the comics as well no  pictures Jesse Pinkman Breaking Bad  began with the likeable protagonist and  over the course of five seasons turned  him into a strangely likable villain  while the show mostly focused on Walter  White it also dealt with the moral  situations White's family faced and  Walters young partner in crime Jesse  Pinkman played by Aaron Paul originally  Jesse was supposed to meet the Reaper at  the end of season 1 thereby accelerating  Walter White's transformation into a  ruthless drug lord legend has it that  the 2007-2008 Writers strike which  shortens Breaking Bad's first season and  forced plot changes saved Jesse but the  strike really just gave creator Vince  Gilligan time to reconsider Aaron Paul  was just too great to let go it became  pretty clear early on that that would be  a huge colossal mistake to kill off  Jesse Jack Shepherd perhaps nobody  benefited more from the narrative  aimlessness of lost than its star  Matthew Fox who played primary  protagonist Jack Shepherd executive  producer JJ Abrams originally planned to  introduce the clear obvious lead in the  first episode only to sacrifice him by  episodes end Abrams thought this would  keep audiences on their  toes and even cast Michael Keaton in the  role who was thrilled with the idea of  getting a network TV role without having  to commit to the entire series Keaton  eventually backed out when showrunners  decided that the character would be kept  alive as Keaton explained to Access  Hollywood an hour show every week I've  got stuff to do I've got a life to lead  and when I fish  [Music]  han Solo Harrison Ford has long seemed  ambivalent toward his most famous role  Star Wars iconic Han Solo it seems like  a minor miracle that Solo survived as  long as he did with Ford ruthlessly  pushing for his own character's demise  during the original series Solo was left  ambiguously frozen at the end of the  Empire Strikes Back specifically because  nobody knew whether Ford would come back  for a third installment when he was  coaxed to return he lobbied George Lucas  unsuccessfully for solos noble sacrifice  in Return of the Jedi  George didn't think there was any future  in dead han toys of course spoiler alert  the character finally did meet his end  in the force awakens and if any of the  many fan theories are correct it may  have been just the noble sacrifice that  Ford always wanted Ellen Ripley 1979's  alien was one of the first genre films  to feature a strong female lead but in  production Sigourney Weaver's Ellen  Ripley wasn't necessarily central to the  film speaking with Entertainment Weekly  director Ridley Scott revealed his  brutal alternate ending for alien the  alien pulls up the door she Harper lives  and makes no difference  it comes forward and it slams through  her masker rips her head off then the  creature would have called the ground  crew saying I'm signing off in the  captain's voice  fortunately Scott thought better of the  silly idea allowing Ripley to live on to  become an even more badass character in  an even better film this is ripley last  survivor of the Nostromo signing off  nebula in guardians of the galaxy vol 2  nebula played by Karen Gillan revealed  herself to be a more complex character  than the jealousy driven villain she  first appeared to be but she almost  didn't get to stay in the MCU having  been written out at the end of the first  guardians film speaking with screen  crush Gillan said nebula was saved by  rewrites she revealed that her final  scene was never actually shot saying he  never got to that point as soon as we  would get near to it there would be a  noose  Gillan also appeared in Avengers  infinity war and will definitely be in  it's untitled sequel for which she can  thank guardians writer director James  Gunn and his indecisiveness anyways  happy trails  mr. Spock Leonard Nimoy had a famously  complicated relationship with his most  famous character mr. Spock he told the  producers of 1979 Star Trek the motion  picture that he was only going to play  Spock one last time so the character  wasn't even included in the first draft  of the screenplay for Star Trek 2 the  Wrath of Khan  but then screenwriter Jack be so words  had an idea why not give Spock an epic  final scene live long and prosper  Nimoy liked the idea but subsequent  rewrites pushed Spock's final scene  farther and farther back until it was at  the end of the movie  Nimoy approved each draft and loved  sowards writing so much that it made him  want to keep playing Spock which he did  for another 27 years  [Music]
 

